<<<
4, Wrong repetition after or.
===
4. Wrong repetition after *or*.
>>>

<<<
1. Or)(nor. There are
===
1. *Or*)(*nor*. There are
>>>

<<<
whether or or nor is used.
===
whether *or* or *nor* is used.
>>>

<<<
with I can neither read nor write (in which nor is requisite), & with I cannot either read or write (in which or is requisite), I cannot read nor (or indifferently or) write.
===
with *I can neither read nor write* (in which *nor* is requisite), & with *I cannot either read or write* (in which *or* is requisite), *I cannot read nor* (or indifferently *or*) *write*.
>>>

<<<
Z cannot read, nor (can I) write; I cannot read(-)or(-) write, where the supposed hyphens mean that write may be substituted for read if desired.
===
*I cannot read, nor* (can I) *write*; *I cannot read*(-)*or*(-) *write*, where the supposed hyphens mean that *write* may be substituted for *read* if desired.
>>>

<<<
The use of nor in such cases was formerly in fashion, & that of or is now in fashion; that is all.
===
The use of *nor* in such cases was formerly in fashion, & that of *or* is now in fashion; that is all.
>>>

<<<
But the modern preference for or where it is equally legitimate with nor has led to its being preferred also where it is illegitimate; so:—
===
But the modern preference for *or* where it is equally legitimate with *nor* has led to its being preferred also where it is illegitimate; so:—
>>>

<<<
It is of great importance that they should face them in no academic spirit, or trust too much to conclusions drawn from maps./No Government Department or any other Authority has assisted.
===
*It is of great importance that they should face them* in no academic spirit, or *trust too much to conclusions drawn from maps.*/*No Government Department or any other Authority has assisted.*
>>>

<<<
The test of legitimacy has been explained in Nor; & it suffices here to say that in the first extract it is the position of no (alter to they should not face them in any), & in
===
The test of legitimacy has been explained in ^^NOR^^; & it suffices here to say that in the first extract it is the position of *no* (alter to *they should not face them in any*), & in
>>>

<<<
the second the presence of any (precluding the carrying on of no), that forbid or.
===
the second the presence of *any* (precluding the carrying on of *no*), that forbid *or*.
>>>

<<<
2. Number, pronouns, &c., after or. When
===
2. Number, pronouns, &c., after *or*. When
>>>

<<<
in the extract below, account should be accounts; for discussion see NuMBER, 8:
===
in the extract below, *account* should be *accounts*; for discussion see ^^NUMBER^^, 3:
>>>

<<<
Either the call of patriotism & the opportunity of seeing new lands, or conscription, or the fact that tramping was discouraged even by old patrons when the call for men became urgent, account for it.
===
*Either the call of patriotism & the opportunity of seeing new lands, or conscription, or the fact that tramping was discouraged even by old patrons when the call for men became urgent,* account *for it.*
>>>

<<<
If alternative members differ in number &c., the nearest prevails (Were you or he, was he or you, there? ; either he or you were, either you or he was), but some forms
===
If alternative members differ in number &c., the nearest prevails (*Were you or he, was he or you, there?; either he or you were, either you or he was*), but some forms
>>>

<<<
+ | (e.g. Was I or you on duty?) are avoided by inserting a second verb (Was I, or were you ...?).
===
+ (e.g. *Was I or you on duty?*) are avoided by inserting a second verb (*Was I, or were you . . . ?*).
>>>

<<<
pronouns (A landlord or landlady expects their, his or her, his, rent) are usually avoided, their rent or the rent due to them being ungrammatical,
===
pronouns (*A landlord or landlady expects their, his or her, his, rent*) are usually avoided, *their rent* or *the rent due to them* being ungrammatical,
>>>

<<<
his or her rent or the rent due to him or her clumsy, & his rent or the rent due to him slovenly; some evasion, as expecis tent, or the rent, is always possible.
===
*his or her rent* or *the rent due to him or her* clumsy, & *his rent* or *the rent due to him* slovenly; some evasion, as *expects rent,* or *the rent,* is always possible.
>>>

<<<
3. Or in enumerations. JI never heard a sermon that was simpler, sounder, or dealt with more practicai matters.
===
3. *Or* in enumerations. *I never heard a sermon that was simpler, sounder, or dealt with more practical matters.*
>>>

<<<
there is a confusion between two correct ways of saying the thing, viz (a) that was simpler, sounder, or more practical, (b) that was simpler or sounder or dealt with more practical matters.
===
there is a confusion between two correct ways of saying the thing, viz (a) *that was simpler, sounder, or more practical,* (b) *that was simpler or sounder or dealt with more practical matters.*
>>>

<<<
See ENUMERATION, & for full discussion AND 2.
===
See ^^ENUMERATION^^, & for full discussion ^^AND^^ 2.
>>>

<<<
he should have inserted and in the place indicated:
===
he should have inserted *and* in the place indicated:
>>>

<<<
A few years ago the natural instincts of the other Powers would have been to notify Italy that Tripoli was a Turkishpossession, \ thatif she wished
===
*A few years ago the natural instincts of the other Powers would have been to notify Italy that Tripoli was a Turkish possession,* ‸ *that if she wished
>>>

<<<
to secure it she must do so by negotiation & purchase, or, failing that, put her case before a conference of the Powers.
===
to secure it she must do so by negotiation & purchase, or, failing that, put her case before a conference of the Powers.*
>>>

