4. Wrong repetition after *or*. A misguided
>>>

<<<
illustrated in the article OvERZEAL.
===
illustrated in the article ^^OVERZEAL^^.
>>>

<<<
It is peculiarly common with or, & to put writers on their guard
===
It is peculiarly common with *or*, & to put writers on their guard
>>>

<<<
False analogy from and explains it; with and, it does not matter whether we say without falsehood & deceit or without falsehood & without deccit, except
===
False analogy from *and* explains it; with *and,* it does not matter whether we say *without falsehood & deceit* or *without falsehood & without deccit,* except
